course: dictionary
lesson: 3123
title: Dangerous words: electrocute
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words warn you about danger, and the sentence around them shows what the danger is. «Never use mains electrical things near a full bath or sink, because a device falling in can electrocute the person in the water.» The clues are falling in and the person in the water, so electrocute is about a shock from electricity.
explain: Electrocute means to hurt or kill a person or animal with a strong electric shock. Try your meaning in the sentence: «a device falling in can give the person in the water a strong electric shock» still makes sense, so you have it right.
explain: Some sentences say what is not true. «A phone charger in bed will not electrocute you» tells you the charger is not deadly in that way. The real danger comes next: a phone under a pillow or duvet can overheat and start a fire, so charge it on a hard surface.
explain: Safety words are serious, so if you are unsure, check a dictionary. Keep electrical things away from water, and never touch a plug with wet hands.
fact: Electrocute means to hurt or kill a person or animal with a strong electric shock.
fact: Electrocute is a verb, so it changes: electrocutes, electrocuted, electrocuting.
fact: Be electrocuted and get electrocuted both mean to receive a deadly electric shock.
fact: Keep mains electrical things such as hair dryers and chargers away from full baths and sinks, because water and electricity are a dangerous mix.
fact: Never put fingers or objects into sockets, and never touch a plug with wet hands.
exercise: «Never use mains electrical things near a full bath or sink, because a device falling in can electrocute the person in the water.» What does electrocute mean? | To dry something with hot air | To wash someone in warm water | *To hurt or kill someone with a strong electric shock | To fall over in the bath
exercise: Which rule about electricity keeps you safe? | Touch a fallen power line to move it out of the way | Climb a pylon to get a better view | Put a hair dryer in the bath to warm the water | *Keep electrical things away from water
exercise: Which word is the past tense of electrocute? | electrocute | electrocutes | *electrocuted | electrocuting
test: «A phone charger in bed will not electrocute you, but a phone under a pillow can overheat and start a fire.» Why should you charge a phone on a hard surface? | So it is easier to reach the plug | To stop the screen getting dirty | To make it charge faster | *So it does not overheat and start a fire
test: Which situation could electrocute someone? | *A hair dryer dropped into a full bath | A cup of tea on a table | A dog walking by the sea | A book left on a bed
exam: Ella says a phone charger in bed will electrocute you. Is she right? | *No, the sentence says it will not electrocute you, but the phone can overheat | No, because electrocute only means to tickle someone | Yes, because pillows carry electricity | Yes, every charger in bed is deadly
